# The same header reached through different spellings from different TUs
# collapses to ONE replacement in the merged output, keyed by the file's
# canonical absolute path rather than by the FilePath string.
#
# This is the defect in issue #48: a.c includes "inc/shared.h" and records
# './inc/shared.h'; sub/b.c reaches the same header via -I/abs/inc and records
# '/abs/inc/shared.h'. Deduplicating by string kept both, and
# clang-apply-replacements applied both to one file, producing
# `__attribute__((noescape)) __attribute__((noescape))`. (The extractor's
# real edits are zero-length insertions; zero-length-insert-canonical-path.test
# has that shape with the issue's exact two spellings. This test uses a
# non-zero length so the edits also pass through the conflict-cluster
# machinery, which zero-length insertions bypass.)
#
# Three spellings of one file, each targeting a different resolution step:
#   a.yaml  absolute path                     (already canonical)
#   b.yaml  relative path with a `..` segment (needs cwd + normalization)
#   c.yaml  symlink                           (needs real_path, not merely
#                                              make_absolute)
# All three carry the byte-identical edit, so the expected outcome is a
# silent collapse — the same treatment the pre-dedup pass already gives
# byte-identical replacements whose FilePath strings happen to agree.
#
# Each input also names the same main source file, main.c, by the matching
# three spellings; MainSourceFile is canonicalized before the inputs are
# compared, so they agree and the merged document carries its absolute path
# rather than the '' that disagreeing inputs produce.

# RUN: rm -rf %t.dir
# RUN: mkdir -p %t.dir/sub
# RUN: printf '%%80s' '' > %t.dir/foo.cpp
# RUN: printf '%%80s' '' > %t.dir/main.c
# RUN: ln -s foo.cpp %t.dir/link.cpp
# RUN: ln -s main.c %t.dir/link-main.c

# RUN: cp %S/Inputs/single-replacement.tpl.yaml %t.dir/a.yaml
# RUN: cp %S/Inputs/single-replacement.tpl.yaml %t.dir/b.yaml
# RUN: cp %S/Inputs/single-replacement.tpl.yaml %t.dir/c.yaml
# (One -e per sed: under lit, BSD sed binds -i's suffix to the next word.)
# RUN: sed -i'' -e "s|MainSourceFile:  '@FOO@'|MainSourceFile:  '%/t.dir/main.c'|; s|@FOO@|%/t.dir/foo.cpp|g" %t.dir/a.yaml
# RUN: sed -i'' -e "s|MainSourceFile:  '@FOO@'|MainSourceFile:  'sub/../main.c'|; s|@FOO@|sub/../foo.cpp|g" %t.dir/b.yaml
# RUN: sed -i'' -e "s|MainSourceFile:  '@FOO@'|MainSourceFile:  'link-main.c'|; s|@FOO@|link.cpp|g" %t.dir/c.yaml

# The relative spellings resolve against the merger's working directory.
# RUN: cd %t.dir && clang-ssaf-src-edit-merge -o %t.dir/merged.yaml \
# RUN:     %t.dir/a.yaml %t.dir/b.yaml %t.dir/c.yaml 2> %t.dir/stderr.txt

# Every path in the document is asserted as a spelled-out literal — not
# matched with a wildcard — because a wildcard could not tell link.cpp from
# foo.cpp, or the `..` spelling from the resolved one, and so could not see
# which spelling won or whether canonicalization ran at all. The literals
# assume %t contains no symlinked component (real_path would resolve one and
# the literal would not match). The pre-existing tests in this suite do not
# make that assumption — they match only a basename suffix, or `{{.*}}` —
# so the canonical-path tests added for issue #48 are the ones that do. On a
# build tree reached through a symlink, they fail together for that reason.
# RUN: FileCheck --input-file=%t.dir/merged.yaml -DDIR=%/t.dir %s
# CHECK:      MainSourceFile:  '[[DIR]]/main.c'
# CHECK-NEXT: Replacements:
# CHECK-NEXT:   - FilePath:        '[[DIR]]/foo.cpp'
# CHECK-NEXT:     Offset:          10
# CHECK-NEXT:     Length:          3
# CHECK-NEXT:     ReplacementText: X
# CHECK-NEXT: ...
# CHECK-NOT: FilePath:

# Silent: no conflict line, no missing-file line.
# RUN: FileCheck --input-file=%t.dir/stderr.txt --allow-empty --check-prefix=NOSTDERR %s
# NOSTDERR-NOT: {{conflict:|does not exist}}
